      "text": "Given that \\(\\det \\mathbf{M} = -2\\), we need to find the determinant of \\(\\mathbf{M}^4\\).\n\nBy the properties of determinants, we know that for any square matrix \\(\\mathbf{M}\\), the determinant of the matrix raised to a positive integer power is the determinant of the matrix raised to that power. Specifically, for any matrix \\(\\mathbf{M}\\) and positive integer \\(k\\),\n\\[\n\\det (\\mathbf{M}^k) = (\\det \\mathbf{M})^k.\n\\]\nIn this case, we are given that \\(\\mathbf{M}^4\\), and thus \\(k = 4\\). Therefore, we need to compute \\((\\det \\mathbf{M})^4\\). Substituting the given value of \\(\\det \\mathbf{M}\\),\n\\[\n\\det (\\mathbf{M}^4) = (\\det \\mathbf{M})^4 = (-2)^4.\n\\]\nCalculating \\((-2)^4\\),\n\\[\n(-2)^4 = (-2) \\times (-2) \\times (-2) \\times (-2) = 16.\n\\]\nThus, the determinant of \\(\\mathbf{M}^4\\) is \\(\\boxed{16}\\).",
